Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:24.370 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  metroid is a nintendo institution one  that dates back almost as far as the  company's console business but it's been  in a funk for the past decade and losing  favor along the way fans don't want  experimental spin-offs like Metroid  Prime Federation force they want to  explore creepy alien worlds as Samus  Aran and hunt for secret and high-tech  equipment  finally with Metroid Samus returns that  call has been answered  now why it took Nintendo's so long to  get to this point is anyone's guess but  Samus Returns is so good that it almost  doesn't matter a reimagining of the  offline Game Boy game Metroid to the  return of Samus Samus Returns is classic  Metroid at heart as in the 1991  monochromatic classic you hunt down  dozens of powerful Metroid's on planet  sr388 in an effort to eradicate the  bioweapon species and keep them out of  Evil's hands however two key changes  have occurred the map on the planet has  been greatly expanded and reshaped to  more closely resemble what you might  find in Super Metroid and combat is more  of a priority than ever  the latter is an effect of Nintendo  bringing on Mercury's team to develop  the game thankfully and most importantly  Sheamus returns feels like a Nintendo  made Metroid but it's still easy to spot  mercury seems influenced for the better  the most immediate contribution that you  see is samus's new parry action a first  for the series that allows you to  counter-attack and stun a rushing  opponent in turn common enemies mostly  alien wildlife are more aggressive than  usual more liable to seek you out than  wait for you to make the first move  though appearing feels a bit strange at  first as it brings your momentum to a  temporary halt you quickly learn the  proper timing and understand how it fits  into your repertoire and win to rely on  it you can also fire in any direction  now thanks to the 3d S's analog stick  all you need to do when surrounded by  enemies is hold another button to stand  your ground and aim freely samus's  newfound flexibility and physicality  makes her feel like an even more capable  hero and makes the moment-to-moment  exploration more lively than usual  considering that metroid is more or less  the foundation of so-called Metroidvania  games where you wander massive  environments poking and prodding walls  and ceilings to reveal secret chambers  and items it's both curious and exciting  when you unlock samus's scanned pulse  ability triggering a pulse both reveals  map layouts and information including  hidden passages and temporarily  highlights breakable objects in your  environment you no longer need to waste  time looking for secrets that may not  exist  conversely Samus Returns makes the  process of acquiring items you've  located more difficult than usual  you're now challenged to quickly juggle  weapons abilities and maneuvers without  faltering to reach items picked up  during your scans there's a healthy  balance between easy pickups and these  more puzzling scenarios and compared to  other 2d metroids it's far more  fulfilling to work smarter rather than  harder to reach a hundred percent item  completion the real metroid endgame  [Music]  by and large Samus returns does a great  job of providing insight into samus's  ever growing capabilities giving you the  information you need to overcome most  puzzles or obstacles there is however  one isolated blemish in this regard a  maneuver with inconsistent behavior  depending on a very specific  circumstance that's never mentioned or  hinted at whether by design or by  accident this exception flies in the  face of the game's otherwise clear and  informative nature and proves  frustrating in a few specific and  punishing locations upgrades aside the  all-important Metroid battles are the  other star of the show and you will  encounter over 40 of them during your  mission at varying stages of the species  evolution you initially battle with  alpha Metroid's the first step beyond  the familiar jellyfish form without  advanced weapons and defenses at the  start you will still struggle a little  while they dive bomb you from overhead  but they're always exposed weak points  do make them easy targets the next few  evolutions are notably more powerful  capped off with omega Metroid's towering  quadrupedal beasts that quickly climb  walls and spew damaging fireballs  granted why these bosses are more  involved and enjoyable than fighting  common enemies there comes a time when  facing even omegas stops being really  excited but Samus returns has some  tricks up its sleeve  introducing a few surprise battles that  help break up the action overall and  subtly reinforce Metroid two's critical  link to the rest of the series  samus returns story and lore will  resonate with anyone who's familiar with  chozo the origin of Metroid's and samus  his role in the future for anyone else  the implications therein will likely fly  over their heads  but smartly mercury steam in intend o  have elegantly incorporated these  details so as to not distract an  uninterested player Samus returns is at  its best when you are engaged in  exploration in common and thus sink  deeper into the planet and into  isolation these are things anyone can  joy and the game never lets teasing and  pleasing fans getting away as the first  2d Metroid game in over a decade  samus returns faces unfairly high  expectations in hindsight there was  never anything to worry about  and a lot to look forward to Sheamus  Returns is both a return to form and a  look to the potential future for 2d  Metroid games where combat plays a  bigger role and exploration involves  clever thinking rather than persistent  guessing fans get more than they  bargained for and everyone else gets a  great 2d action game with one of the  most captivating and capable video game  heroes around  [Music]  [Applause]

